Aligned.

I think D.9a resolves the objective question cleanly and I support it.

The completion-test criterion is significantly stronger than treating objective as a rendering exception.

I also agree that the distinction is:

- determined by the input
vs
- not determined by the input

rather than:

- objective exists
vs
- objective does not exist

The "no new nouns" rule is especially valuable because it makes the population of objective auditable rather than plausibility-based.

The examples all feel correctly classified under D.9a.

I also agree with the observation that demanding a separable purpose for self-bounding artefacts introduces noise into the field that downstream readers are most likely to optimize against.

The narrower question about the artefact's missing subject is much better than attempting to discover a higher-level rationale.

D.11a looks good as well.

For the remaining items:

1. I confirm the definition-slot list as:

- objective
- audience
- environment
- form

and therefore open: remains bounded to those dimensions.

2. I would drop the echo-rate threshold from the specification.

I do not currently expect there to be an owner responsible for collecting and reviewing that metric.

I would rather keep only the structural safeguards that are enforceable by the prompt itself:

- fixed example banks
- generic examples
- spanning examples
- at least one clearly out-of-domain example
- no generated examples

A metric without ownership risks appearing stronger than it actually is.

One UX preference before consolidation:

For the rendered examples, I would like them displayed vertically rather than inline.

Current:

Examples of the kind of answer:
businesses · internal employees · retail investors · students

Preferred:

Examples of the kind of answer:

• businesses
• internal employees
• retail investors
• students

Similarly:

Examples of the kind of answer:

• a decision is made
• a document exists
• a process runs without you
• someone's behaviour changes

My reasoning is readability.

At this point the examples are explicitly framed as examples rather than options, defaults, recommendations, or menus, so I am no longer concerned about list formatting making them appear selectable.

The vertical layout is easier to scan and will likely become more important if individual examples grow beyond a few words.

Aside from that, I consider the architecture closed.

Please proceed with the full consolidated specification with all approved changes folded in.
